mbia an  investigation is underway after two  catholic churches were destroyed in  overnight fires  the churches were located about 40  kilometers apart on penticton and  soyuz indian banned lands officials say  both of the fires began about the same  time early this morning  they've not said whether they believe  the two are connected though police say  they are treating both fires as  suspicious ahead why there's going to be  an  awkward silence in the stands at the  olympics in tokyo  the summer olympics have already been  delayed by a year  spectators from foreign countries have  been told they're not welcome  but today the organizers said some  japanese spectators will be allowed  though  they won't be able to cheer redmond  shannon reports on the ever-evolving  rules around the tokyo olympics  the covet modified olympic village is  ready for athletes  and organizers say they are ready for  fans  venues will now be allowed a spectator  capacity of 50  up to a maximum of 10 000 people well i  gotta tell you my reaction this morning  when i heard that was  yay like um and and i have to do it  quietly because it's pretty much what  i'm gonna have to do when i'm in tokyo  that's because team canada's chef de  mission marnie mcbean  and everyone else at the arenas will not  be allowed  to cheer i think to golf i think to  tennis  i think to live theater that a quiet  audience can give incredible gravitas to  a moment  and for sure you know what people are  gonna still do they're gonna gasp  and they're gonna clap and they're gonna  stop the three-time olympic champion  says her athletes are just  happy to be competing after the one-year  delay  even if no canadian fans will be allowed  to travel  i know it's it's not perfect nothing has  been  over the last 18 months but there will  be witnesses there will be people  present to share in the energy in the  moment  the ioc says not everyone in the olympic  bubble  will be vaccinated for the residents of  the olympic villages it will be  well above 80 percent so far only  one in six japanese people have received  a vaccine dose  and japanese prime minister yoshihide  suga warns that if infections in the  country rise again  events may yet happen without any fans  polls suggest most japanese people want  the games postponed  or cancelled redmond shannon global news  london  a weightlifter from new zealand will  make history as the first transgender  athlete to compete  at an olympic games  that's laurel hubbard she will compete  in the women's super heavyweight 87  kilogram plus category  the 43 year old had participated in  men's weightlifting competitions before  transitioning  in 2013. in 2015 the international  olympic committee updated qualifying  requirements for transgender athletes  the rules allow women to compete as long  as their testosterone levels  meet requirements we do know that there  are many questions about fairness of  transgender athletes competing  in the olympic games but i would like to  take this opportunity to remind us all  that  morale has met all of the required  criteria  she is a very dedicated and resilient  athlete  up next an indigenous writer's words of  wisdom become  a best-seller  [Music]  an indigenous writer from winnipeg has  landed a major literary accolade  her book is called i sang you down from  the stars it tells the story of a cree  mother preparing for the birth of her  child  in late april it debuted at number three  on the new york times  best sellers list here's tasha spillet  sumner talking about her book  and what inspired her to write it ice  began to melt we visited the river i've  always admired picture books but  now with my with my daughters as a new  inspiration to write  own voice picture books in which our  children can see themselves and our  families and community  communities reflected in beautiful  dignified ways on the  page i started writing i sing you down  from the stars when i first found out i  was pregnant with isabella  i wanted to tell the story that our  teachings hold about how  children come to our families so the  story and nice singing down from the  stars is  a really a love song between a mother  and a child  in that time when she's hoping and  wishing for the child and then preparing  for the child to come  into her family's lives as an author i  feel like it's my responsibility and  part of my gift to write into reality  a world that is worthy of my daughter  that is worthy of all of our children  and each of us do that in our own way i  picked up a small stone for you  indigenous people come from such  beautiful strong teachings that have  kept our nation strong and alive through  you know some of the most horrific  things and of course we know that  nationally indigenous communities are  grieving from the  um from the revealing of so many  unmarked children  graves and so i think about particularly  now  as we hold our children in our arms it's  so important that we do everything that  we can  to ensure that they still have a  connection to that ancestral beauties  through strength to keep to keep our  nations going to keep our families  structures going  tasha spill it sumner and that is global  national for this monday i'm donna  friesen we leave you tonight with a  long-awaited change to a park  in halifax  local officials and migma leaders  gathered to unveil the peace and  friendship  park the park had originally been named  after the founder of halifax  edward cordon wallace who placed  bounties on mgmaw  men women and children the mayor says  the new name symbolizes the desire to  work together to recognize a wrong and  not be  bound by history but rather informed by  its lessons  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow  take care of yourselves bye-bye  you
 

